-- allium: 3
--
-- Reach — how far a player gets, and what they would find next
--
-- Scope
--   The first of four models of a player, and the one in which a limit
--   hides things. A deduction the player does not know, cannot hold, cannot
--   take in or cannot read is not there for them, and that is the whole of
--   it: what is left is found at no cost and never wrongly. Put to a set of
--   givens it says how far such a player gets, by which techniques, and
--   where they stop. Put to a puzzle in play it says what that player would
--   find next, which is a hint pitched at them. effort.allium,
--   lapse.allium and human-solving.allium model the same player differently
--   and none of the four imports another. A player described in
--   human-solving.allium's finer terms is put to this model through the
--   projection it states.
--   Source: docs/explanation/solving-sudoku.md, for the workflow a
--   systematic player follows.
--
-- Includes
--   - The run: one for each rating or hint asked for. It looks, steps, and
--     looks again, until the grid is full or nothing it can see is left.
--   - Sight: the four ways a profile hides a deduction, which are
--     technique.allium's knows, holds, takes_in and can_read.
--   - Choice: a systematic player takes the lowest technique on the ladder
--     that yields anything, an unsystematic one whatever is seen, and either
--     is drawn first to what their fixation favours.
--   - Steps: the deductions taken, in order, which are the trace a rating
--     reads and the one step a hint is.
--   - Three boundaries: Rating and Hinting, where a run is asked for, and
--     RunResult, where it is read.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Cost. A step here is free, and a puzzle is within reach or it is not.
--     What it takes out of a player is effort.allium's.
--   - Error and guessing. Marks are kept true, every step is sound, and a
--     player who sees nothing stalls. A novice who would guess is reported
--     here as stalled; what guessing does is lapse.allium's. So this model
--     reads repertoire, capacity, spans, marking, order and fixation, and
--     ignores upkeep, budget, fatigue and patience.
--   - Which of several equally preferred deductions is taken. It must be the
--     same each time and is otherwise the implementation's, as the order of
--     tied branches is in solver.allium.
--   - How a hint is worded or shown, and anything a surface draws.
--   - Chance. The same profile and the same digits give the same run.
--
-- Dependencies
--   sudoku.allium, for Given and Puzzle, and technique.allium, for the grid,
--   the catalogue, deductions and the profile.
--
-- Vocabulary
--   technique.allium's words stand. To them: run. Look, the moment a run
--   surveys what its grid licenses; step, a deduction taken. See, of a
--   deduction the profile does not hide. Stall, to look and see nothing with
--   the grid unfilled. Rating and hint, the two things a run is for.

use "./sudoku.allium" as sudoku
use "./technique.allium" as technique

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum Purpose { rating | hint }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Run {
    profile: technique/Profile
    purpose: Purpose

    -- The digits the run starts from: givens for a rating, and for a hint
    -- every digit standing in the puzzle, the player's with the setter's.
    start: Set<sudoku/Given>

    status: looking | stepping | solved | stalled | answered

    -- A run looks and steps by turns. A rating ends solved or stalled. A
    -- hint ends on its first look: answered with one step, or stalled if
    -- this player would see nothing. It is never solved, since a puzzle that
    -- is unsolved and consistent has a cell to fill.
    transitions status {
        looking -> stepping
        stepping -> looking
        looking -> solved
        looking -> stalled
        looking -> answered
        terminal: solved, stalled, answered
    }

    -- The run is the grid's keeper. Absent only between the run's creation
    -- and the rule that opens its grid.
    grid: technique/Grid with keeper = this

    steps: Step with run = this
    last_steps: steps where is_last

    -- The miss model entire: a deduction is seen when nothing about the
    -- profile hides it. Known, small enough to hold, narrow enough to take
    -- in, and read from marks only by a player who keeps them.
    sees(x): profile.sees(x)

    sees_something: grid.deductions.any(x => sees(x))

    -- Systematic: nothing seen is lower on the ladder. Every step is a
    -- fresh look, which is the return to the cheap techniques the source
    -- asks for. Unsystematic: anything seen will do.
    is_in_order(x):
        sees(x)
            and (profile.order = unsystematic
                or grid.deductions.all(o => sees(o) implies o.technique.rank >= x.technique.rank))

    -- Whether the player's eye is already there. Nearby is a cell of the
    -- deduction sharing a row, a column or a box with a cell of the last
    -- step; same_digit is a digit shared with it. Nothing is favoured before
    -- the first step or by a player with no fixation.
    is_favoured(x):
        last_steps.any(s =>
            (profile.fixation = same_digit
                and x.digits.any(d => d in s.deduction.digits))
            or (profile.fixation = nearby
                and x.cells.any(p => s.deduction.cells.any(q =>
                    p.row = q.row or p.column = q.column
                        or (box_index(p.row, config.box_side) = box_index(q.row, config.box_side)
                            and box_index(p.column, config.box_side) = box_index(q.column, config.box_side))))))

    -- Fixation bends the order and never breaks it: a favoured deduction is
    -- preferred among those in order, and where none is favoured all of them
    -- are. So fixation cannot stall a run that order would have moved.
    is_preferred(x):
        is_in_order(x)
            and (is_favoured(x)
                or not grid.deductions.any(o => is_in_order(o) and is_favoured(o)))

    -- Ready to look: the grid stands whole and every mark is true.
    is_poised: status = looking and grid != null and grid.is_kept
}

entity Step {
    run: Run

    -- From 1, in the order taken.
    index: Integer

    deduction: technique/Deduction

    is_last: run.steps.all(o => o.index <= index)

    -- As high on the ladder as any step of the run. What a rating reports as
    -- the hardest technique the puzzle asked of this player.
    is_hardest: run.steps.all(o => o.deduction.technique.rank <= deduction.technique.rank)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    box_side: Integer = sudoku/config.box_side
    side: Integer = sudoku/config.side
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Beginning -------------------------------------------------

rule BeginRating {
    when: Rate(givens, profile)

    -- A rating is of a puzzle, and only well-posed givens are one. As
    -- sudoku.allium's SetPuzzle requires, in its words; solver.allium's
    -- verdict of one says the same.
    requires: solution_count(givens, config.side) = 1

    ensures: Run.created(profile: profile, purpose: rating, start: givens, status: looking)
}

rule BeginHint {
    when: NextStep(puzzle, profile)

    -- No hint is offered over a conflict: the player has something to put
    -- right first, and a grid in conflict licenses nothing that can be
    -- trusted. standing_digits is a black box: every filled cell of the
    -- puzzle as a position and its digit, given or placed.
    requires: puzzle.status = unsolved
    requires: puzzle.is_consistent

    ensures: Run.created(
        profile: profile,
        purpose: hint,
        start: standing_digits(puzzle),
        status: looking
    )
}

rule OpenGrid {
    when: run: Run.created

    -- technique.allium lays the grid out when it is created.
    ensures: technique/Grid.created(
        keeper: run, start: run.start, uniqueness_promised: run.purpose = rating
    )
}

-- Upkeep ----------------------------------------------------

rule KeepMarksTrue {
    when: cell: technique/GridCell.has_stale_candidate

    -- Only of a grid a run of this model keeps, and only while it is
    -- looking: a finished run's grid is never touched again. A grid is born
    -- kept, so a candidate only ever turns stale when a step places a digit,
    -- and this answers each cell that step has made stale.
    for run in Runs where grid = cell.grid:
        requires: run.status = looking

        -- In this model upkeep is perfect and free: whatever a placed peer
        -- rules out is struck before the run looks again. A player who keeps
        -- no marks is modelled the same way and simply cannot read them.
        ensures: cell.candidates = cell.candidates - cell.placed_peer_digits
}

-- Looking and stepping --------------------------------------

rule Look {
    when: run: Run.is_poised

    requires: run.status = looking
    requires: run.is_poised

    -- Read before anything changes, so that the step about to be recorded
    -- is not counted as the last one or among the steps. chosen of nothing
    -- is nothing, and then no step is recorded.
    let taken = chosen(filter(run.grid.deductions, x => run.is_preferred(x)))
    let next_index = run.steps.count + 1

    -- One rule and three ends, in this order. A full grid is solved; a full
    -- grid licenses nothing, so this comes first. A grid the player sees
    -- nothing in is where they stall. Otherwise one preferred deduction is
    -- taken. chosen is solver.allium's black box: one member of the
    -- collection, the same whenever the collection is the same. filter is a
    -- black box: the members of the collection the condition holds of.
    ensures:
        if run.grid.is_filled:
            run.status = solved
        else if not run.sees_something:
            run.status = stalled
        else:
            Step.created(run: run, index: next_index, deduction: taken)
            if run.purpose = hint:
                run.status = answered
            else:
                run.status = stepping
}

rule TakeStep {
    when: run: Run.status transitions_to stepping

    requires: run.status = stepping

    -- The step Look has just recorded is applied as technique.allium pins
    -- it: its placement put in its cell, or each of its strikes taken from
    -- its cell. A hint never gets here, so its one step is recorded and not
    -- taken: the puzzle is the player's to change. A placed digit then
    -- leaves its peers by KeepMarksTrue, and the run looks again once it
    -- has.
    ensures:
        run.status = looking
        for step in run.last_steps:
            if step.deduction.placement != null:
                for cell in run.grid.cells:
                    if cell.row = step.deduction.placement.position.row
                            and cell.column = step.deduction.placement.position.column:
                        cell.digit = step.deduction.placement.digit
                        cell.candidates = { step.deduction.placement.digit }
            for strike in step.deduction.strikes:
                for cell in run.grid.cells:
                    if cell.row = strike.position.row and cell.column = strike.position.column:
                        cell.candidates = cell.candidates - strike.digits
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- These hold in every state a run can reach. Two properties compare one
-- state with the next and so are prose. A systematic run never takes a step
-- while a lower technique it can see yields anything: Look reads the ladder
-- afresh each time. And a run never undoes anything: a placed digit stays
-- and a struck candidate stays struck, because nothing here is a guess.

invariant EveryStepWasSeen {
    for step in Steps:
        step.run.sees(step.deduction)
}

invariant StepsAreNumberedInOrder {
    for a in Steps:
        for b in Steps:
            a != b and a.run = b.run implies a.index != b.index
}

invariant StepsCountFromOne {
    for step in Steps:
        step.index >= 1 and step.index <= step.run.steps.count
}

invariant AHintIsOneStep {
    for run in Runs:
        (run.purpose = hint implies run.steps.count <= 1)
            and (run.status = answered implies run.purpose = hint and run.steps.count = 1)
}

invariant SolvedMeansFilled {
    for run in Runs:
        run.status = solved implies run.grid.is_filled
}

-- The grid of a finished run is never touched again, so this stays true.
invariant StalledSeesNothing {
    for run in Runs:
        run.status = stalled implies not run.grid.is_filled and not run.sees_something
}

-- Of a rating, whose grid is laid out from givens and kept true. A hint's
-- grid is the player's, and a digit that fits and is wrong can leave it
-- with a cell that allows nothing; what a hint does then is the open
-- question below.
invariant NoRatingIsContradictory {
    for run in Runs:
        run.purpose = rating and run.grid != null implies not run.grid.is_contradictory
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Where a rating is asked for: a generator grading what it has made, or a
-- setter checking who a puzzle is for.
surface Rating {
    provides:
        Rate(givens, profile)

    @guarantee AlwaysEnds
        -- Every run ends. Each step fills a cell or strikes a candidate that
        -- stood, nothing is ever put back, and a grid holds a finite number
        -- of both.

    @guarantee NeverWrong
        -- The grid starts true and KeepMarksTrue strikes only what the rules
        -- forbid, so by technique.allium's DeductionsAreSound every step is
        -- sound. A solved run's grid is the puzzle's one solution, and a
        -- stalled run's grid is part of it.

    @guarantee StalledIsNotUnsolvable
        -- Stalled says this profile sees nothing more. It says nothing of
        -- the puzzle, which is well-posed, and nothing of what the player
        -- would do next. The grid as far as it got is there to be read, and
        -- so is every technique the catalogue licenses that the profile hid.

    @guarantee SameInputSameRun
        -- The same givens and the same profile give the same steps in the
        -- same order and the same end, every time.

    @guarantee LimitsOnlyHide
        -- A profile changes which deductions are seen and in what order they
        -- are taken. It never changes what a deduction is, what it costs or
        -- whether it is right.
}

-- Where a hint is asked for: the surface a puzzle is played on.
surface Hinting {
    context puzzle: sudoku/Puzzle

    provides:
        NextStep(puzzle, profile)
            when puzzle.status = unsolved and puzzle.is_consistent

    @guarantee AHintIsWhatThisPlayerWouldFind
        -- The step a hint run records is the first this profile would take
        -- from the same digits and premises: a technique the player knows,
        -- of a size they can hold, where they would look. Unverified player
        -- entries carry no uniqueness promise, so a hint cannot use the two
        -- uniqueness techniques that a rating run may use. A hint is never
        -- drawn from above the profile, and a stalled hint run says there
        -- is nothing at this level under the available premises.

    @guarantee AHintChangesNothing
        -- A hint run reads the puzzle and never places in it. Its grid is
        -- its own.
}

-- What a run has to show for itself. It draws nothing and words nothing.
surface RunResult {
    context run: Run

    exposes:
        run.purpose
        run.status
        run.profile
        for step in run.steps:
            step.index
            step.deduction
            step.is_last
            step.is_hardest
        for cell in run.grid.cells:
            cell.row
            cell.column
            cell.digit
            cell.candidates
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "A hint over a digit that fits and is wrong: the puzzle is consistent, so NextStep is offered, but the grid is no longer true and a licensed deduction may be unsound or the grid may contradict. Should the hint say the digit is wrong, using the solution, or refuse?"

open question "Does a richer profile always reach at least as far? It does if every order ends at the same grid, which holds for a systematic player with every span; whether a fixation or a missing span can close off a deduction for good is not shown. technique.allium's ExtraCandidatesHideAndNeverMislead now says outright that it is no monotonicity claim: past subsets, a strike one player takes can change the shape another pattern needed, so the answer may be no even for a systematic player."

open question "Should a rating of a stalled run name the lowest technique that would have moved it, as effort.allium does by escalating, or is that effort.allium's alone?"
